   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to extending the authority of the department of environmental conservation to manage lobster",
    "basePrintNo": "S5005",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5005-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0329, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5005\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law, in\nrelation to extending the authority of the department of environmental\nconservation to manage lobster\n\nPurpose of the Bill:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the authority of the Department\nof Environmental Conservation (DEC) to manage lobsters.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nSection 1 of this bill amends Environmental Conservation Law (ECL)\n13-0329(16) to extend, until December 31, 2018, DEC's authority to\nadopt regulations to implement conservation measures that affect take,\npossession and landings of lobsters (Homarus americanus) in Atlantic\nStates Marine Fisheries Commission (ASMFC) Areas One, Two, Three,\nFour, Five and Outer Cape , consistent with the recommendations of the\nrespective Area's Lobster Conservation Management Teams as required by\nthe Interstate Fishery Management Plan (FMP) for American Lobster\nadopted by ASMFC.\n\nSection 2 of this bill provides that the bill shall take effect\nimmediately.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nECL § 13-0329(16) grants DEC regulatory authority, until December 31,\n2015, for lobsters in ASMFC Areas One, Two, Three, Four, Five and\nOuter Cape.\n\nPrior Legislative History:\n\nDEC's regulatory authority to manage lobsters in ASMFC Areas One, Two,\nThree, Four, Five and Outer Cape was last extended by Chapter 439 of\nthe Laws of 2011.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nDEC's regulatory authority for lobster is necessary in order to\npreserve the current regulatory program. Failure to extend the\nexisting regulatory authority by December 31, 2015 would result in the\nloss of regulatory protection for lobsters in Areas One, Two, Three,\nFour, Five and Outer Cape. Regulatory authority is critical for New\nYork to maintain compliance with the ASMFC FMP for American Lobster.\nRegulatory authority provides the mechanism necessary to react rapidly\nto the ever-changing FMP in a timely fashion. Moreover, failure to\nmake necessary regulatory changes as may be required by the FMP could\nresult in the closure of New York's lobster fishery.\n\nBudget implications:\n\nNone.\n\nLocal impact:\n\n\nThis bill would have no impact on local governments.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
